+## kotlin-extensions
+
+**High**
+- `modules/kotlin/extensions/.../flows/FlowChunkingExtensions.kt:34-36,28,45`. Every chunk is the same `MutableList`, cleared straight after `emit`, so any collector that keeps a chunk sees it emptied or overwritten. Scenario: `flowOf(1,2,3,4).chunk(2).toList()` returns `[[], []]` (traced by reading the code; no test covers it). There are more problems in the same function:
+  - `original` is reassigned on every `collect`, so collecting the flow a second time stacks `onCompletion` handlers that flush into the first collector.
+  - The timed flush emits from an `async` child coroutine, which breaks the flow rule that emissions come from the collecting coroutine (`SafeCollector` throws) and races on `chunk`.
+  - Empty lists are emitted when the upstream is empty and on idle ticks.
+
+  Confidence: confirmed. Fix: emit `chunk.toList()`, keep state local to `collect`, and do time-based flushing with `channelFlow` and a `Mutex`/`select`.
+
+**Medium**
+- `.../async/DeferredExtensions.kt:13-21`. `awaitAny` does not cancel the others when a deferred fails. If any deferred completes exceptionally, `select` throws, `.also { …cancelAndJoin() }` is skipped, and the rest keep running, which contradicts "cancels the rest". Confidence: confirmed. Fix: wrap in `try/finally` and cancel the still-active deferreds in `finally`.
+
+**Low**
+- `.../collections/ListExtensions.kt:6`. `index` lives outside the `sequence {}`, so all iterators of the same sequence share it. Scenario: `s.take(2).toList()` called twice on `[a,b,c]` gives `[a,b]` then `[c,a]`. An empty list throws `IndexOutOfBounds`. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: move `var index` inside the `sequence` block.
+- `.../number/NumberExtensions.kt:5,7`. `pow` goes through `Double`. Scenario: `3L.pow(39)` is off by 11, and `Int.pow` saturates at `Int.MAX_VALUE` instead of failing. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: use `BigInteger.pow` or integer multiplication with overflow checks.
+- `.../bytes/BytesExtensions.kt:6`. `requiredBits` is wrong for powers of two and for 0. Scenario: `8.requiredBits == 3` (it needs 4) and `0.requiredBits == Int.MIN_VALUE`. Nothing uses it. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: `Int.SIZE_BITS - Integer.numberOfLeadingZeros(this)`, or delete it.
