+# swissknife — cryptography, protected-value, jwt, keycloak, security, sql
+
+## cryptography
+
+**High**
+- `modules/cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/src/main/kotlin/.../symmetric/encryption/aes/AESKeyAdapter.kt:50`: `toString()` prints the raw AES key bytes (`encoded=${encoded.contentToString()}`). Logging a `SymmetricKey`, or anything that contains one, writes the secret key to the log. `SymmetricKeyWithEncapsulation.toString()` (`cryptography/domain/.../symmetric/SymmetricKeyWithEncapsulation.kt:23`) leaks it the same way through `key=$key`. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: return a masked string such as `AESKeyAdapter(algorithm, format, hash)`.
+
+**Low**
+- `cryptography/domain/.../asymmetric/signing/SigningPrivateKey.kt:11`: `fun <OPTIONS> SigningPrivateKey.sign(input: String, ...)` declares a type parameter that nothing uses, so it can never be inferred. `key.sign("text")` fails to compile with "Not enough information to infer type variable OPTIONS". Confidence: confirmed (Kotlin inference rule; it has no callers). Fix: delete `<OPTIONS>`.
+- `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/.../utils/BouncyCastleUtils.kt:27` and `AESKeyAdapter.kt:58`: signing uses a fresh `SecureRandom()`, and non-XTS AES key generation calls `KeyGenerator.init(length)` with no random source. The `SecureRandom` injected into `CryptographicOperations.bouncyCastle(random)` is silently ignored on both paths, so a caller-chosen RNG (deterministic tests, a DRBG policy) does not apply. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: pass `random` into `initSign(key, random)` and `keyGenerator.init(length, random)`.
+- `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/.../asymmetric/signing/JavaVerifyingPublicKeyAdapter.kt:15`: `verify(input, signature)` takes the JCA algorithm name from `signature.metadata.algorithmName`, which arrives with the untrusted signature. A tampered or foreign algorithm name makes verification throw (NoSuchAlgorithm or InvalidKey) instead of returning `false`. Confidence: plausible. Fix: verify with `key.algorithm` and reject a mismatched metadata algorithm by returning `false`.
+- `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/.../asymmetric/signing/JavaSigningKeyAdapter.kt:17`: `Signature.Metadata.keyHash` is the hash of the **private** key. A verifier, who only has the public key, cannot use it to look up the verifying key. The spec at `CryptographyTestSpecification.kt:114` pins this behaviour. Confidence: plausible (design defect). Fix: put the public key's hash in the metadata.
+- `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/.../xts/XtsAes.kt:102-107`: there is no check that the data key and tweak key differ. IEEE 1619-2018 and FIPS require K1 ≠ K2, and an all-same key such as a 32-byte key with equal halves is accepted. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: `require(!dataKey.encoded.contentEquals(tweakKey.encoded))`.
+- `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/src/test/.../XtsAesTests.kt:72-101`: ciphertext stealing is only round-trip tested. No IEEE 1619 vector of non-block-aligned length (vectors 15–18) is checked, so a self-consistent but non-standard stealing implementation would pass. I traced the stealing logic by hand and it is correct today. Confidence: confirmed (test gap). Fix: add IEEE vectors 15–18 as known-answer tests.
+- `cryptography/domain/README.md:3` and `cryptography/implementation/bouncy-castle/README.md:3`: both describe "CTR mode". The code only provides GCM and XTS. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: update the READMEs.
+
+## protected-value
+
+**Medium**
+- `protected-value/domain/.../ProtectedValueFactory.kt:9`, used at `factory/aes/.../GcmAes256ProtectedValueFactory.kt:17`: the public `protectValue(..., iv: ByteArray?)` passes a caller-chosen IV straight to GCM, with no length check and no uniqueness guarantee. Any caller who passes a fixed or derived IV for one owner key reuses the GCM nonce, which exposes the XOR of the plaintexts and lets an attacker forge authentication tags. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: remove the `iv` parameter and always use `encryptWithRandomIV`.
+- `factory/aes/.../GcmAes256ProtectedValueFactory.kt:17,30`: the value's `name` and `owner` are never bound as associated data. Decryption replays `metadata.associatedData` from the same untrusted envelope, which binds nothing. A ciphertext can be swapped between fields (for example "email" into "ssn") or between owners that share a key, and it decrypts without error. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: encrypt and decrypt with AAD = serialized `(name, owner)`, rebuilt from the value and not taken from the metadata.
+- `protected-value/domain/.../ProtectedValueData.kt:12` and `domain/README.md:3`: `access(context)` never uses `context`; it is only implied by the log line. `unprotect` has no context parameter, so no implementation can enforce access. The README promises "access-controlled decryption". Confidence: confirmed. Fix: pass `context` to `unprotect` (or into an authorizer), or remove the claim.
+
+**Low**
+- `factory/aes/src/test/.../AesProtectedValueTests.kt:38`: `assertThat(protectedValue.value).isNotEqualTo(originalValue)` compares a `ByteArray` with a `String`, so it can never fail. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: compare against `originalValue.toByteArray()` with a content check.
+- `protected-value/factory/aes/README.md:3`: says "AES-CTR-256", but the implementation is AES-256-GCM. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: update the README.
+
+## jwt
+
+**Medium**
+- `jwt/jose4j/processor/.../JoseJwtAdapter.kt:16` (also `:14`, `:11-12`): `issuedAt` and `issuerId` are evaluated eagerly with no null handling. I checked with jshell that `JwtClaims().getIssuedAt()` and `getIssuer()` return null when the claim is absent. Two failures follow:
+  - `readAndVerify` throws a `NullPointerException` for a validly signed token without `iat` when `requireIssuedAt=false`.
+  - `readWithoutVerifying` throws a `NullPointerException` for any token missing `iss` or `iat`.
+
+  `id` and `subject` also throw a `NullPointerException` when read if the claim is missing. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: make these properties nullable, or evaluate them lazily with explicit errors.
+- `jwt/jose4j/processor/.../JwtProcessorFactory.kt:36-43`: `newJKSJwtProcessor` takes no `Configuration`. It sets no `requireExpirationTime`, no subject or iat requirement, no JWS algorithm allow-list and no clock skew, and it skips audience validation. Tokens without `exp`, which never expire, are accepted from the JWKS issuer. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: accept a `configuration` and apply the same builder settings as `newJwtProcessor`.
+
+**Low**
+- `JwtProcessorFactory.kt:20`: `newJwtProcessor` always calls `setSkipDefaultAudienceValidation()`, so a token the issuer minted for service A is accepted by service B. Confidence: plausible (it may be intended, but it is undocumented). Fix: add an optional expected audience to the configuration.
+- `JwtProcessorFactory.kt:45-66`: the audience-specific processor never calls `setEnableRequireEncryption()`, so a plain signed JWS is accepted where a JWE is expected. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: call `builder.setEnableRequireEncryption()`.
+- `jwt/domain/.../JWT.kt:27`: `isValidAtTime` ignores `notBeforeTime`, so a token whose `nbf` is in the future is reported as valid. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: also require `notBeforeTime == null || notBeforeTime <= time`.
+- `jwt/jose4j/examples/src/test/.../JwtExampleTests.kt`: only happy paths are tested. Nothing checks rejection of expired tokens, a wrong key or signature, the wrong issuer or audience, a tampered payload, or `alg` outside the allow-list. Confidence: confirmed (test gap). Fix: add negative cases.
+
+## keycloak
+
+**Medium**
+- `keycloak/container/.../KeycloakContainerExtensions.kt:20`: `adminClient(..., password = ...)` calls `.password(adminPassword)`, which ignores the `password` argument. Logging in as any non-default user with an explicit password authenticates with the container admin password and fails. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: `.password(password)`.
+
+**Low**
+- `keycloak/container/.../Keycloak.kt:21-26`: `customize()` runs before the defaults are applied, so any admin username/password, startup attempts/timeout or RAM settings made in `customize` are overwritten. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: apply `customize()` last.
+- `keycloak/container/src/test/.../KeycloakExampleTests.kt:82,142-143,90`: this `@Disabled` test can never pass if re-enabled:
+  - `delay(timeout)` equals the test timeout, so it always times out.
+  - Clients are created with only `name` set (no `clientId`), then looked up with `clients().get(representation.clientId)`, but that call needs the internal UUID.
+  - `authzClient` passes `client.id` (the UUID) as the OAuth `clientId`.
+
+  Confidence: confirmed. Fix: set `clientId`, use `get(representation.id)`, pass `representation.clientId` to `AuthzClient`, and drop the `delay`.
+
+## security (scan)
+
+**Medium**
+- `security/scan/.../TrivyImageScanner.kt:85`: `return@repeat` inside `repeat {}` behaves like `continue`, not `break`. A scan that is "not worth retrying" (missing image) is still re-run `maximumAttempts` times, just without the delay. The KDoc and comment say it is not retried, and the tests only cover `isWorthRetrying`, not the loop. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: use a `for` loop with `break`, or throw `TrivyScanFailed` straight away.
+
+**Low**
+- `security/scan/.../DockerImageSecurityAssertions.kt:43`: `staleAcceptedIds` is computed against a scan filtered by `severities`. An accepted CVE that is still present but below the threshold (for example MEDIUM) is announced as "NO LONGER present — remove from .trivyignore". Confidence: confirmed. Fix: scan all severities for the stale check, or word the message as "not found at the scanned severities".
+- `security/scan/README.md:3`: refers to `assertDockerImageHasNoVulnerabilities()`, which does not exist; the real API is `assertThatImage(...).hasNoUnacceptableVulnerabilities()`. `DockerImageSecurityAssertions.kt:19-20` cites a Gradle `securityScan` convention that sets `securityScan.trivyIgnoreFile`, and nothing in the workspace sets it. This fails closed. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: correct the docs.
+
+## sql
+
+**High**
+- `sql/postgres/migrator/liquibase/.../CustomLockService.kt:26-39`: this is a check-then-act race. When two instances both see the lock held by a dead session, A calls `releaseLock()` and acquires the lock in `super.waitForLock()`. B then calls `releaseLock()`, Liquibase's unconditional `UPDATE ... SET LOCKED=false WHERE ID=1`, which wipes A's live lock. B acquires too, and both run migrations concurrently. This is exactly the case of several pods restarting after a crash. Confidence: plausible (traced; not reproduced). Fix: release with a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE ID=1 AND LOCKEDBY=<dead session value>` instead of `releaseLock()`.
+
+**Medium**
+- `CustomLockDatabaseChangeLogGenerator.kt:48` together with `CustomLockService.kt:43`: the locker's `backend_start` is stored as `Timestamp.toString()`, a JVM-local-time string with no zone. Liveness is checked with `BACKEND_START='<string>'` interpreted in the checking session's TimeZone. If instances run with different JVM timezones, the active locker is judged dead and its lock is released, so migrations run concurrently. The SQL is also built by string interpolation. Confidence: plausible. Fix: store `extract(epoch from backend_start)` (or an ISO instant with offset) and compare with a bound parameter.
+- `sql/reactive/utils/.../WithSqlConnectivity.kt:46`: `bindValueOrNull` binds every null as `String`, i.e. a VARCHAR-typed parameter. In Postgres, `INSERT`/`UPDATE` of a null into an integer, uuid, timestamptz or jsonb column fails with "column is of type X but expression is of type character varying". The `selectPage` bindings go through this path. Confidence: plausible. Fix: let the caller pass the null's type, e.g. `bindNull(key, type)`.
+
+**Low**
+- `sql/reactive/utils/.../StatementUtils.kt:15`: the indexed `bindNullable` uses `T::class.java`, which for `Int`, `Long` or `Boolean` is the primitive class. r2dbc-postgresql has no null codec for primitives, so this throws "Cannot encode null parameter of type int". The named variant correctly uses `javaObjectType`. Confidence: plausible. Fix: use `T::class.javaObjectType`.
+- `sql/postgres/container/.../PostgresContainer.kt:70`: `connectionOptions(rootUser = true)` connects as role `root` with the app user's password. The postgres image creates no `root` role (the superuser is `POSTGRES_USER`), so authentication always fails. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: drop the flag, or use the configured username as the superuser.
+- `sql/postgres/utils/.../PostgresReadinessCheck.kt:27`: `catch (error: Exception)` swallows `CancellationException`, so a cancelled readiness check returns `Failed` instead of propagating cancellation. Confidence: confirmed. Fix: rethrow `CancellationException` before the generic catch.
+- `sql/reactive/utils/.../RowParsingUtils.kt:9`: `booleanValue` casts the column to `Byte`. Postgres via R2DBC returns `Boolean`, so this always throws `ClassCastException` (no callers today). Confidence: confirmed. Fix: `getValue<Boolean>(columnName)`.
+- `sql/migrator/liquibase/.../LiquibaseDatabaseMigrator.kt:29,38,45`, three problems with the lazy connection:
+  - `jdbcConnection` is evaluated as an argument before `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)`, so the blocking connect runs on the caller's dispatcher.
+  - `stop()` on a migrator that never ran opens a DB connection just to close it, and throws if the DB is unreachable.
+  - `database.use {}` closes the connection, so a second `applyMigrations()` fails.
+
+  Confidence: confirmed. Fix: open the connection inside `withContext(IO)` per run, and make `stop()` a no-op if it was never initialised.
+- `sql/postgres/pagination/utils/.../SqlEntityFilter.kt:33-46`: the count and page queries run concurrently on separate connections outside a transaction. Under concurrent writes, `Pagination.Information.count` and the continuation-token decision (which compares `size == count` in the real `RequirementsFilter`) come from different snapshots, so a token can be omitted early or point at an empty page. Confidence: plausible. Fix: run both in one repeatable-read transaction, or document that the count is approximate.
+- There are no tests for the custom Liquibase lock service, pagination, or the statement/binding helpers; the only test in the sql modules is `SqlConnectionOptionsTests`. Confidence: confirmed (test gap). Fix: add Testcontainers tests for lock takeover and null binding.
+
+**Outside the slice but affects crypto:** `hashing/utils/.../blake3/Blake3.kt` has a leftover `println("HashBytesLength...")`. It fires on every `CryptographicKey.hash`, signature and `GCM.Metadata` creation, writing to stdout.
+
+**Coverage:** I read every main, test, README and build file in cryptography, protected-value, jwt, keycloak, security and sql. I checked callers in pillar and element-service-example (none in modulith-example), checked jose4j and BouncyCastle behaviour in jshell, and ran `:protected-value-factory-aes:test` (3/3 passing).
