+# swissknife — messaging, pulsar, nats, avro, json, logger, logging, opentelemetry, test
+
+## Bug hunt: swissknife messaging, pulsar, nats, avro, json, compliance-checker, logger, logging, opentelemetry, test
+
+The most serious finding is in pulsar: Avro consumers ignore the version of the schema a message was written with, so schema evolution is broken whenever a consumer reads data written with another schema version. Next are the SLF4J adapter dropping exceptions from the common `log("msg {}", arg, ex)` call, topic parsing silently truncating names, and the NATS consumer subscribing again on every collection.
+
+Nothing was edited. I checked the key claims with `jshell` against the real jars (Avro 1.12.2, org.json, slf4j 2.0.19) and with `javap` against the Pulsar 4.2.4 jars. I didn't run any Gradle tests.
+
+### pulsar
+
+**High**
+- `pulsar/avro/serialization/.../AvroReader.kt:9` — `AvroReader` only implements `read(bytes, offset, length)` and `read(InputStream)`. It doesn't override `read(bytes, schemaVersion)` and doesn't forward `setSchemaInfoProvider`. Pulsar's `AbstractStructSchema.decode(bytes, version)` calls `reader.read(bytes, version)`, and the interface default for that drops the version. So `MultiVersionGenericAvroReader` is dead code and every message is decoded as if the consumer's own schema were the writer schema.
+  - Failure: after a compatible schema change (say v2 adds an optional field), a v2 consumer reading v1 messages hits EOF and gets a `SchemaSerializationException`. If fields were reordered or removed, values are silently garbled.
+  - Confidence: confirmed (Pulsar bytecode checked).
+  - Fix: override `read(ByteArray, ByteArray)`, `read(InputStream, ByteArray)` and `setSchemaInfoProvider` to delegate to `reader`.
+- `pulsar/messaging/adapter/.../PulsarPartitionChangeAwareMessageConsumer.kt:18` — Every `newMessageConsumer` (Avro and JSON utils included) forces `.subscriptionType(Failover)` after the caller's `customize`.
+  - Failure: a service that passes `Shared` or `Key_Shared` to scale out silently runs Failover, so only one consumer is active per partition.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: drop the forced `subscriptionType` and leave it to `defaultConsumerCustomization`.
+
+**Medium**
+- `PulsarPartitionChangeAwareMessageConsumer.kt:34,40` — The listener uses a `MutableSharedFlow()` with no replay or buffer, and emits through `runBlocking` on Pulsar's listener thread.
+  - Failure: the initial `becameActive` fires during the lazy `subscribe()`, before anyone collects, so it is lost. When there is a collector, Pulsar's internal thread blocks until the collector finishes.
+  - Confidence: plausible.
+  - Fix: use `MutableSharedFlow(replay = 64)` and `tryEmit`.
+- `pulsar/messaging/adapter/.../protocol/PulsarReceivedMessage.kt:31` — `removeFromLast("-partition")` cuts at the last occurrence of `-partition`.
+  - Failure: a non-partitioned topic such as `.../user-partitioning` is reported (and stored in parent-id lineage) as `.../user`.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: use `TopicName.get(topicName).partitionedTopicName`.
