+# element-service-example — root, shared, contracts, service, migrator, chart, configuration
+
+# element-service-example bug hunt: root, shared, contracts, service, migrator, chart, configuration
+
+**Bottom line:** two critical defects block any Kubernetes deploy. The migrator image cannot start on its JDK 26 base, and the Helm deployment template renders an env value that isn't a string. Both get past the build because nothing tests the migrator image or renders the chart. Everything here comes from reading the code and its dependencies; nothing was built or deployed.
+
+Root for every path below: `/Users/msollecito/coding/workspace/element-service-example`.
+
+## Deployment (Jib images and Helm chart)
+
+**Critical**
+- `content/migrator/postgres/starter/build.gradle.kts:42`: the migrator's `-XX:+ZGenerational` flag no longer exists on JDK 26, and the base image is `eclipse-temurin:26-jre-noble` (`gradle.properties:19`).
+  - Failure: I ran local Temurin 26.0.1 with this flag. It prints `Unrecognized VM option 'ZGenerational'` and the JVM refuses to start. So the `postgres-migrator` init container exits at once, no app pod ever starts, and nothing tests the migrator image.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: delete `-XX:+ZGenerational` (and `-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions`).
+- `content/chart/templates/deployment.yaml:63`: `value: { { .Values.log.format | default "json" } }` has spaces inside the braces, so it is a YAML flow mapping, not a template action.
+  - Failure: the env var value renders as a nested map instead of a string, so Kubernetes rejects the Deployment and `helm install` fails.
+  - Confidence: confirmed (from YAML semantics; no `helm` on this machine to render it).
+  - Fix: `value: {{ .Values.log.format | default "json" | quote }}`.
+
+**Medium**
+- `content/chart/values.yaml:12,19` (plus `values.yaml:15,21`, `Chart.yaml:6`, `deployment.yaml:37,46`): the chart pulls `registry.tools.acme.info/.../example` and `.../example-postgres-migrator` at tag `latest`.
+  - Failure: the build only produces `ghcr.io/sollecitom/element-example-service` and `ghcr.io/sollecitom/element-service-example-postgres-migrator`, tagged with the git hash, so the chart deploys images this repo never builds. Because the tag is always `latest`, `helm upgrade` produces an identical pod spec, so no rollout happens. App and migrator versions can also drift apart.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: point the repositories at the Jib image names and set `image.tag`/`migrator.image.tag` to the same git hash.
+- `content/chart/values.yaml:90-106` with `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:70-75`: the health server starts only after Postgres and Pulsar checks pass and all modules have started.
+  - Failure: there is no startupProbe, and liveness gives 10s + 3×5s = 25s. The Pulsar client allows 30s connection/operation timeouts. A slow broker or DB on startup gets the pod killed before `/liveness` exists, which can turn into a crash loop.
+  - Confidence: plausible.
+  - Fix: add a `startupProbe` on `/liveness` with a budget of at least 60s.
+- `content/service/test/security-scan/build.gradle.kts:11`: Trivy scans only the service image.
+  - Failure: the migrator image, which shares the base and is deployed as the init container, is never scanned. CVEs in it, including whatever the `.trivyignore` edit concerns, go unnoticed.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: add the migrator image to the scan.
+
+**Low**
+- `content/chart/templates/deployment.yaml:60`: `MODULES_HTTP_EVENT_BRIDGE_USER_SERVICE_ADDRESS` is read by nothing (I grepped every module's env keys). It is dead config and gives a false sense that it's wired. Confirmed. Fix: remove it.
+- `content/chart/values.yaml:93,102`: the probe ports are hard-coded to `8081` while `HEALTH_PORT` comes from `service.healthPort`. Changing `healthPort` silently breaks the probes. Confirmed. Fix: template both from `.Values.service.healthPort`.
+- `content/migrator/postgres/starter/build.gradle.kts:33-35,39`: `-Djava.security.egd` is passed as a program argument, which `main` ignores. `HeapDumpPath=.../java_pid<pid>.hprof` uses a literal `<pid>` (HotSpot only expands `%p`). Confirmed. Fix: move the egd flag to `jvmFlags` and use `%p`.
+
+## Service wiring and configuration
+
+**Medium**
+- `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:112`: Pulsar overrides are read with `configurationPropertiesUnderRoot("pulsar.configuration")`. That helper (swissknife `EnvironmentExtensions.kt:59`) strips the prefix before calling `get`, so every value comes back null. Environment variables never match the dotted prefix at all.
+  - Failure: no `pulsar.configuration.*` setting reaches `loadConf`, so TLS/auth for a production broker can't be configured.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: `associate { it.removePrefix("$root.") to get(it) }` (in swissknife).
+- `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:121`: the instance ID is `newId()`, random on every start, and becomes the Pulsar consumer `nodeName`. `INSTANCE_NODE_NAME` (`ServiceProperties.kt:19-20`) is never read.
+  - Failure: Failover subscriptions assign partitions by consumer name, so every restart reshuffles partitions. That is exactly what `README.md:30-34` says the variable prevents.
+  - Confidence: confirmed.
+  - Fix: build `ServiceInfo` from `EnvironmentKey.instanceNodeName(environment)`, and have the chart set it from the pod name.
+- `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:104`: the R2DBC URL is `r2dbc:postgresql://` with no `pool:`, and swissknife's `createR2dbcClient` doesn't wrap it in a pool.
+  - Failure: every query opens a new TLS connection to Azure Postgres. That adds latency and can exhaust `max_connections` under load.
+  - Confidence: plausible.
+  - Fix: use `r2dbc:pool:postgresql://` (r2dbc-pool) or wrap the factory in a `ConnectionPool`.
+
+**Low**
+- `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:80-90`: `stop()` never closes the `PulsarClient` or the R2DBC factory. If one module's `stop()` throws, the remaining modules and the health server are skipped. Confirmed. Fix: close both clients in `finally` and stop modules in reverse order with `runCatching`.
+- `content/service/starter/.../Service.kt:75-76`: the health server (and so readiness) starts before the main HTTP server, leaving a short window where the pod reports ready but port 8080 isn't bound. Plausible. Fix: start `mainHttpDrivingAdapter` first.
+- `content/configuration/src/main/resources/default-configuration.yml`: nothing loads this file (`StandardEnvironment()` is called with `Environment.EMPTY`). Its `port: 0` defaults and `instance.*` values are dead, and `SERVICE_PORT`/`HEALTH_PORT` are actually required lenses. Confirmed. Fix: delete the file, or pass it as `defaultConfiguration`.
+
+## Liquibase migrations
+
+**Medium**
+- `content/migrator/.../1_3_1_recreate_compliance_plan_reports_table.sql:24-30`: `Compliance_Plan_Output_Standards` has no index. The outputs manager deletes from it and pages through it by `(customer_id, compliance_plan_id)` (`StandardsOperations.kt:304,345`), so every list and replace is a sequential scan. Confirmed. Fix: add a changeset with `CREATE INDEX ... ON Compliance_Plan_Output_Standards (customer_id, compliance_plan_id, pk)`.
+- `content/migrator/.../1_4_0_create_compliance_plan_table.sql:1`, `1_4_2_add_requirement_id_column.sql:1`, `1_4_3_add_conditional_requirement_flag_column.sql:1`: schema changes are done with `DROP TABLE ... [CASCADE]` and recreate, with no data copy. Any database upgrading through these changesets loses every compliance plan and requirement, plus user-set `applicable` flags that can't be rebuilt from events. Confirmed. Fix: write future changes as `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN` with a backfill.
+
+## Shared compliance domain and serialization
+
+**Medium**
+- `content/shared/compliance/plan/domain/model/.../standard/Standard.kt:104-120`: `equals`/`hashCode` compare only `reference` and `publication`.
+  - Failure 1: every Avro round-trip test that compares a `Standard`, directly (`StandardAvroSerdeTests`) or inside an event (`CompliancePlanEventAvroSerdeTests`), passes even if `title`, `abstract`, `type`, `testingRequirements`, key functional elements, applicability reasons, `language`, `format` or `applicabilityExplanation` are dropped or corrupted.
